<Legal lang="en" title="Health and safety" path="/en/health">
  <p class="box">
    Finisher provides a general training plan calculated by rules. It is not medical, physiotherapy or nutritional advice and does not replace
    a check-up with a doctor.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you start</h2>
  <p>Talk to a doctor before you start training if you:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>have heart disease, high blood pressure or another chronic condition,</li>
    <li>have ever felt chest pain, dizziness or fainted during exercise,</li>
    <li>are pregnant or have recently given birth,</li>
    <li>have an injury or pain in your joints, bones or muscles,</li>
    <li>are returning after a long break or take medication that affects your heart or blood pressure.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>During training</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Stop and get help if you feel chest pain, unusual breathlessness, dizziness or an irregular heartbeat.</li>
    <li>Pain that gets worse while running or does not go away the next day is a sign to rest and see a professional.</li>
    <li>The plan increases volume by about 10 % per week at most. If you are tired or ill, skip a session rather than catch up.</li>
    <li>Paces are estimates from your goal time and recent result. Adjust them for heat, hills and how you feel.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Race nutrition</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The gel schedule is a calculation from carbohydrates per hour, not nutritional advice.</li>
    <li>Try gels and drinks on long runs first, never for the first time on race day.</li>
    <li>We do not calculate fluids or salt. Drink to thirst and adjust for the weather.</li>
    <li>If you have allergies, diabetes or other dietary needs, talk to a professional.</li>
  </ul>
</Legal>
